<!--
  Directory.Build.targets — applies to every MSBuild project under the repo root.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
  ===============
  Unity regenerates Assembly-CSharp.csproj (and the package csprojs beside it) whenever the asset
  database refreshes, and it bakes ABSOLUTE paths to the Visual Studio Tools for Unity analyzers
  into each one:

      <Analyzer Include="C:\Users\<user>\.vscode\extensions\visualstudiotoolsforunity.vstuc-1.2.2\Analyzers\Microsoft.Unity.Analyzers.dll" />

  The version number is part of that path. When VS Code updates the extension — 1.2.2 to 1.3.1 on
  this machine — the old folder is deleted, but the generated csprojs keep pointing at it until
  Unity next regenerates them. Every `dotnet build` then dies with:

      error CS0006: Metadata file '...vstuc-1.2.2\Analyzers\Microsoft.Unity.Analyzers.dll'
                    could not be found

  That is a broken TOOLCHAIN path, not broken game code, but it is indistinguishable from a real
  compile failure in the output — which is exactly how it took down `tools/verify_steam_layer.py`,
  the gate this project relies on to prove a compile is clean without opening the Editor. The gate
  went red, the code was fine, and nothing in the error text says so.

  It also cannot be fixed by editing the csprojs: they are generated artefacts, `*.csproj` is in
  .gitignore, and Unity overwrites any edit on the next asset refresh. And it cannot be fixed by
  patching only the scratch copy verify_steam_layer.py builds, because the dead reference is
  repeated in the package csprojs that copy pulls in via <ProjectReference>.

  WHAT IT DOES
  ============
  Drops any <Analyzer> whose file is not on disk, immediately before compilation, for every project
  in the tree. Analyzers are advisory diagnostics — losing one degrades lint coverage in an IDE; it
  cannot change the emitted assembly. So dropping a missing one is strictly better than failing the
  build, and an analyzer that IS present is left completely alone.

  This is deliberately scoped to *missing* files. It is not a switch that disables analyzers.

  Unity does not use MSBuild to compile the game (it drives the compiler directly), so nothing here
  affects an Editor or player build. This file only shapes `dotnet build` / IDE builds — which is
  where the failure lives.
-->
<Project>

  <Target Name="OblastZeroDropMissingAnalyzers" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">

    <ItemGroup>
      <Analyzer Remove="@(Analyzer)" Condition="!Exists('%(Identity)')" />
    </ItemGroup>

  </Target>

</Project>
